WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.699
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.699 --> 00:00:12.040
My earliest childhood memories
was playing with Legos.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.824
I loved building Lego cities,
with chips for the pirates, and

00:00:16.824 --> 00:00:21.620
hospitals for the doctors, and
trucks for the firefighters.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:30.625
It was my world of endless possibilities
and I was the architect of my own dreams.

00:00:33.153 --> 00:00:40.421
I can also remember that whenever
I finished building a city,

00:00:40.421 --> 00:00:45.312
I would always have one
piece left over and

00:00:45.312 --> 00:00:48.830
it was always the same piece.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:52.598
It was always the woman.

00:00:54.968 --> 00:01:01.830
Only the woman does not have a uniform,
she did not have a function.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:02.760
She did not have a role.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.680
She was not a chef.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.530
She was not a builder.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:10.840
She was simply a woman.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.950
So I didn't know what to do with her and
I look around my city try to see

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:21.435
where she fit, maybe sicker inside
a house and hope no one would notice.

00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:28.778
Why was the woman the only
character that looked

00:01:28.778 --> 00:01:33.342
like me an afterthought in my own dream?

00:01:36.085 --> 00:01:41.333
I can trace back to this memory my
decision to become an entrepreneur and

00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:45.610
a designer,
because I wanted to play by my own rules.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:51.860
And design gave me the skills and
the tools to build a world of my own.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:58.990
And as a designer, I know that the world
around us is shaped like design choices.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.893
So for that simple reason,

00:02:03.893 --> 00:02:09.930
if we have designed woman out,
we can design woman in.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:13.519
How?

00:02:13.519 --> 00:02:18.513
The first step to jumpstart this
change is to realize that the world

00:02:18.513 --> 00:02:23.970
around us is the result of what
researchers call the generic masculine.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.253
The generic masculine means that we,
you and I,

00:02:31.253 --> 00:02:38.030
we interpret everything as male
unless specified as female.

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:45.622
It means that when we say human,
we mean man.

00:02:45.622 --> 00:02:50.857
And by making men our
normal standard human,

00:02:50.857 --> 00:02:54.726
we erase woman from the picture.

00:02:54.726 --> 00:02:58.729
And her absences are everywhere.

00:02:58.729 --> 00:03:02.612
They are in the history rewrite,
in the policy we pursue,

00:03:02.612 --> 00:03:07.932
in the cities we plan, and the algorithms
we code and in the products we design.

00:03:10.394 --> 00:03:15.655
Her absences can take the shape
of an unthreatening toy,

00:03:15.655 --> 00:03:18.290
but they can also be deadly.

00:03:21.942 --> 00:03:26.703
Women are 17% more likely to
die in a car crash then men

00:03:26.703 --> 00:03:32.130
according to the US Department
of Transportation.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:32.630
Why?

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:38.140
Because cars are designed for
men's bodies.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.070
Car crash test dummies are designed
after men's measurements.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:46.874
And car seat belts are designed for men's
torsos, so they simply do not account for

00:03:46.874 --> 00:03:48.601
breast tissue or pregnancy.

00:03:51.165 --> 00:03:55.942
There are more fetal deaths
from car crashes than

00:03:55.942 --> 00:04:00.729
any other trauma, and
I ask, why is it this way?

00:04:03.298 --> 00:04:10.126
The leading cause of death of women
in the USA is cardiovascular disease,

00:04:10.126 --> 00:04:17.193
yet only 25% of hospital emergency
treatments are given to women, why?

00:04:17.193 --> 00:04:22.330
Because diagnosis criteria were
designed to fit men's symptoms.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:27.390
And unfortunately a woman's heart
attack looks nothing like a man's.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.810
Instead of experiencing chest and
arm pain,

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:37.025
women often experience breathlessness,
stomach pain, nausea, and fatigue.

00:04:38.455 --> 00:04:42.388
Her symptoms are referred to as atypical.

00:04:44.196 --> 00:04:50.901
Let that word sink in, atypical.

00:04:50.901 --> 00:04:56.242
So instead of receiving
the treatment that she needs and

00:04:56.242 --> 00:05:01.126
deserves, she is sent home
with a prescription for

00:05:01.126 --> 00:05:03.980
anxiety, why is it this way?

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.931
And even when bias design does not
kill women, it makes their life so

00:05:10.931 --> 00:05:13.340
much harder, unsafe and tiring.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:20.330
When we take men's perceptions,
patterns, and

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:24.347
behaviors and make them our golden rule,

00:05:24.347 --> 00:05:29.280
when we assume that what he wants,
does and needs,

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:35.274
represents us all,
then our design is flawed from the get go.

00:05:35.274 --> 00:05:42.090
And once you see it, you cannot unsee
it because it is everywhere around us.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:50.340
And since we stand on the Stanford campus,
let's glance at our surroundings.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.880
I see a university campus with
outdoor lighting so dim and

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.810
dispersed that female students who
live here feel unsafe at night and

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:04.870
think twice before choosing their paths.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:08.960
Why didn't Stanford make
their walkways brighter?

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.810
Because lighting was not
designed with woman in mind.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:20.500
The very lengthy and very detailed
Stanford lighting guidelines make it

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:25.746
clear light is meant to orient travelers
and to unify campus architecture.

00:06:25.746 --> 00:06:30.217
And places no emphasis on safety or
security perceptions that are central for

00:06:30.217 --> 00:06:31.740
their female community.

00:06:34.892 --> 00:06:39.345
I can also see a full time executive

00:06:39.345 --> 00:06:44.116
program that is only 25% female.

00:06:44.116 --> 00:06:50.373
Why can't Stanford get more executive
woman to apply and come once admitted?

00:06:50.373 --> 00:06:54.145
What invisible hurdles are still there?

00:06:54.145 --> 00:07:01.005
Since this program targets men and women
over 30, I'd recommend we take a long,

00:07:01.005 --> 00:07:06.297
hard look at childcare services
provided because half of the men

00:07:06.297 --> 00:07:11.508
in our program are fathers, and
only three women are mothers.

00:07:14.297 --> 00:07:17.422
How do the systems in
place fail to account for

00:07:17.422 --> 00:07:23.192
the unique challenges faced by mothers
that fathers surpass mothers seven to one?

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:28.018
Why is it this way?

00:07:31.379 --> 00:07:34.583
I believe we're in the right place and

00:07:34.583 --> 00:07:39.899
in the right time to redesign
a world that also works for women.

00:07:41.298 --> 00:07:47.110
Because we are no longer the children
playing with the Lego sets we were given.

00:07:49.876 --> 00:07:54.840
I invite you all,
to reframe the way that you think of your

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:59.704
role from decision makers in business,
in education,

00:07:59.704 --> 00:08:04.366
and in policy, and
to think of yourself as designer.

00:08:04.366 --> 00:08:08.162
To everyone listening, hear this.

00:08:08.162 --> 00:08:11.629
You are a designer.

00:08:11.629 --> 00:08:17.192
Because whenever you're making a decision
about what business model to build,

00:08:17.192 --> 00:08:23.095
what policy to pursue, or what strategy to
roll out, you are making design choices.

00:08:25.806 --> 00:08:32.393
So, dear designers know
that you are powerful.

00:08:32.393 --> 00:08:37.260
Use your power to advocate for
your user and their needs.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:43.376
All of your users and all of their
needs because they are not in the room

00:08:43.376 --> 00:08:47.735
when you're making a decision about them,
so be their voice.

00:08:50.818 --> 00:08:55.422
This responsibility lies on all of us,
not just on woman and

00:08:55.422 --> 00:09:00.410
it influences all products,
all services, and all policies.

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:09.424
And I asked you to go beyond the business
school questions, why this and why now?

00:09:09.424 --> 00:09:13.212
And ask the most important
design question.

00:09:16.318 --> 00:09:18.683
Why is it this way?

00:09:21.216 --> 00:09:26.248
Leaders of today and leaders of tomorrow,

00:09:26.248 --> 00:09:32.555
how will you design a world
that also works for women?

00:09:32.555 --> 00:09:38.269
[MUSIC]

